{"step_index":80,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T10:15:48Z","content":"**Q1. Derive the expression for Logarithmic Decrement ($\\delta$)**\n\n**Definition:** It is the natural logarithm of the ratio of any two successive amplitudes on the same side of the mean position.\n\n**Derivation:**\n1. The equation for under-damped free vibration is: \n   $x = X e^{-\\zeta \\omega_n t} \\sin(\\omega_d t + \\phi)$\n2. Let amplitude $x_1$ occur at time $t_1$:\n   $x_1 = X e^{-\\zeta \\omega_n t_1}$\n3. The next successive amplitude $x_2$ occurs after one time period $t_d$:\n   $x_2 = X e^{-\\zeta \\omega_n (t_1 + t_d)}$\n4. Take the ratio of $x_1$ to $x_2$:\n   $\\frac{x_1}{x_2} = \\frac{X e^{-\\zeta \\omega_n t_1}}{X e^{-\\zeta \\omega_n (t_1 + t_d)}} = e^{\\zeta \\omega_n t_d}$\n5. Take the natural logarithm ($\\ln$) on both sides:\n   $\\delta = \\ln\\left(\\frac{x_1}{x_2}\\right) = \\zeta \\omega_n t_d$\n6. We know time period $t_d = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega_d}$ and damped frequency $\\omega_d = \\omega_n \\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2}$.\n7. Substitute $t_d$:\n   $\\delta = \\zeta \\omega_n \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega_n \\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2}} \\right)$\n8. **Final Equation:**\n   $\\delta = \\frac{2\\pi \\zeta}{\\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2}}$\n\n***\n\n**Q2. Derive Natural Frequency ($\\omega_n$) by Energy Method**\n\n**Principle:** In a conservative system (no damping), the total energy remains constant. Maximum Kinetic Energy = Maximum Potential Energy.\n$K.E._{max} = P.E._{max}$\n\n**Derivation:**\n1. Let mass $m$ have a simple harmonic displacement: $x = X \\sin(\\omega_n t)$\n2. Velocity $v = \\frac{dx}{dt} = X \\omega_n \\cos(\\omega_n t)$\n3. Maximum velocity occurs when $\\cos(\\omega_n t) = 1$: \n   $V_{max} = X \\omega_n$\n4. Maximum Kinetic Energy (of mass):\n   $K.E._{max} = \\frac{1}{2} m (V_{max})^2 = \\frac{1}{2} m X^2 \\omega_n^2$\n5. Maximum Potential Energy (of spring with stiffness $k$):\n   $P.E._{max} = \\frac{1}{2} k X^2$\n6. Equating both:\n   $\\frac{1}{2} m X^2 \\omega_n^2 = \\frac{1}{2} k X^2$\n7. Cancel $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $X^2$ from both sides:\n   $m \\omega_n^2 = k$\n8. **Final Equation:**\n   $\\omega_n = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}} \\text{ (rad/s)}$\n\n***\n\n**Q3. Derive Turning Moment ($T$) from Piston Effo\n<truncated 1232 bytes>\nta = 1$):** The roots are real and equal. The system returns to its equilibrium position in the shortest possible time without any oscillation.\n3. **Over-damped ($\\zeta > 1$):** The roots are real and distinct. The system is sluggish and slowly returns to its equilibrium position without any oscillation.\n\n***\n\n**Q5. Dynamically Equivalent System**\n\nTo replace a rigid continuous body (like a connecting rod of mass $m$) with two concentrated point masses ($m_1$ and $m_2$) placed at distances $l_1$ and $l_2$ from the Center of Gravity, three exact conditions must be met:\n\n1. **Mass Condition:** The sum of the two point masses must equal the total mass of the rigid body.\n   $m_1 + m_2 = m$\n2. **Center of Gravity Condition:** The center of gravity of the two masses must be at the exact same location as the original rigid body.\n   $m_1 l_1 = m_2 l_2$\n3. **Inertia Condition:** The mass moment of inertia of the two masses about the CG must equal the mass moment of inertia of the original rigid body.\n   $m_1 l_1^2 + m_2 l_2^2 = I$\n\n***\n\n**Q6. Magnification Factor & Transmissibility**\n\n1. **Magnification Factor (M.F.):** It is the ratio of the maximum dynamic amplitude ($X_{max}$) to the static deflection under a static force ($X_{static} = F_0 / k$). \n   $M.F. = \\frac{X_{max}}{X_{static}}$\n2. **Transmissibility ($\\epsilon$):** It is the ratio of the force actually transmitted to the foundation ($F_T$) to the disturbing force applied to the machine ($F_0$).\n   $\\epsilon = \\frac{F_T}{F_0}$\n   *Note: For vibration isolation to be effective, the frequency ratio ($\\frac{\\omega}{\\omega_n}$) must be greater than $\\sqrt{2}$.*\n\n***\n\n**Q7. Coefficient of Fluctuation of Speed & Energy**\n\n1. **Coefficient of Fluctuation of Speed ($C_s$):** The ratio of the maximum fluctuation of speed to the mean speed.\n   $C_s = \\frac{N_{max} - N_{min}}{N_{mean}}$\n2. **Coefficient of Fluctuation of Energy ($C_E$):** The ratio of the maximum fluctuation of energy to the total work done per cycle.\n   $C_E = \\frac{\\Delta E}{\\text{Work done per cycle}}$","thinking":"**Defining the Approach**\n\nI'm focusing on providing precise, exam-ready answers for those seven key topics. I am keeping in mind the need to deliver the content from my unique perspective, as if I'm explaining the concepts myself. The aim is accuracy and clarity above all else, in a way that feels organic.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Exam Precision**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a laser-focused, \"exam dump\" style. No conversational padding or pleasantries here. The goal is precise answers that match the exact question format, with high mathematical clarity. This current phase involves crafting answers as per the required GTU exam format with the requisite equations. I'm iteratively refining each answer. I am currently beginning with Q1, regarding Logarithmic Decrement.\n\n\n**Calculating Logarithmic Decrement**\n\nI've just finished the derivation for logarithmic decrement, building upon the damped vibration equation and taking into account the time difference. I ensured each step is logically sound, and I've now produced the final formula, ready for direct application in answering exam questions. The derivation relies on the ratio of amplitudes and simplification through the natural logarithm, ultimately revealing a direct relationship between damping ratio and decrement.\n\n\n**Deriving Moment and Frequency**\n\nI've just finished the derivation of turning moment (T) from piston effort ($F_p$), showing how tangential force and crank radius contribute. I've also verified the natural frequency derivation $\\omega_n = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}}$, making sure each step is mathematically sound. I'm focusing on completeness and accuracy, using consistent notation and clear logical steps, while taking care to avoid omissions.\n\n\n**Visualizing Damping Concepts**\n\nI've just visualized damping concepts. I've sketched the time-displacement graphs for under, critical, and over-damped systems. I'm focusing on the behavior of each with an emphasis on the distinction between oscillatory and non-oscillatory responses, and the rate of decay.\n\n**Defining Equivalent System Masses**\n\nI've just clarified the core conditions for dynamic equivalence: total mass, unaltered center of gravity, and identical mass moment of inertia. I have clearly defined the three criteria using the required variables, emphasizing that these equations provide the foundation for substitution and analysis.\n\n**Calculating Magnification and Transmissibility**\n\nI am now focusing on the magnification factor and transmissibility. I'm focusing on their key definitions and practical application. I am starting by deriving the equations and exploring the factors influencing vibration amplitude.\n\n\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
