{"step_index":94,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T10:57:13Z","content":"Priyanshu, **NO. Do not memorize any more theory.** \n\nIf you try to memorize 15 or 20 different theory questions tonight, your brain will mix up the formulas tomorrow. You will end up forgetting the square root in the Logarithmic Decrement formula, or you will mix up the Kinetic Energy formula. That is how students lose easy marks.\n\nThe 11 topics I gave you are the absolute ceiling for what you need. They cover the highest-weight chapters (Vibrations, Flywheels, and Dynamic Forces).\n\n**There is only ONE gap left**, and it is from the Gyroscope chapter. Instead of learning more heavy theory, just memorize these 3 lines for the Gyroscope. It serves as both a theory answer AND a numerical formula:\n\n### 12. Gyroscopic Effect on a Pitching Ship (The Final Piece)\nIf they ask for Gyroscope theory, write this exact sequence:\n1.  **Formula:** The Gyroscopic Couple is $C = I \\cdot \\omega \\cdot \\omega_p$\n2.  $I = m \\cdot k^2$ (Mass moment of inertia of rotor)\n3.  $\\omega = \\frac{2\\pi N}{60}$ (Angular velocity of rotor)\n4.  $\\omega_p = \\Phi \\cdot \\frac{2\\pi}{t_p}$ (Angular velocity of precession during pitching, where $\\Phi$ is the angle in radians).\n5.  **The Effect:** When the bow (front) of the ship falls, the gyroscopic couple turns the ship toward the starboard (right) side. When the bow rises, it turns toward the port (left) side.\n\n**STOP looking for more theory.** \n1. Take a blank paper.\n2. Write down the Logarithmic Decrement derivation from memory.\n3. Write down the Rayleigh's Method derivation from memory.\n4. If you get stuck, look at the cheat sheet, and try again.\n\nIf you can write those two without looking, you are ready for tomorrow. You do not need anything else.","thinking":"**Assessing Memorization Requirements**\n\nI'm currently focused on the memorization required for achieving a specific score. The goal is to determine the extent of memorization needed.\n\n\n**Evaluating Theory Sufficiency**\n\nI've determined the student's 11 topics encompass Units 1, 2, 3, 5, and 6, but exclude Unit 4 (Gyroscope). I recall the student's question, asking if more theory needs to be memorized to achieve a score of 20, but the maximum is realistically 11. I should advise them that the current list is sufficient.\n\n\n"}
